{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01204 - MONDO_18437",
  "verbalization": "Mitoxantrone, a蒽醌-based anthracycline antibiotic, is implicated in the treatment of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through its ability to inhibit topoisomerase II, a critical enzyme in DNA replication and repair. This mechanism is supported by its association with Therapy Related Acute Myeloid Leukemia and Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia, both of which are linked to Idarubicin, a related anthracycline. The shared pathway involving Idarubicin and AML with Npm1 somatic mutations suggests a potential role for Mitoxantrone in targeting this subset of AML cases. The evidence from the three paths indicates that Mitoxantrone may be repurposed for AML with Npm1 mutations, as it is used in conjunction with Idarubicin, which itself is a known therapeutic agent for this condition. The clinical and molecular evidence points to a mechanistic link between Mitoxantrone and the disease, though the exact extent of its therapeutic effect remains to be fully elucidated.",
